Resolve the merge conflicts from merging `origin/{{.Base}}` into this branch.
The merge has already been run in this worktree and stopped on conflicts.

Run `git status` to see the conflicted files. Resolve every conflict marker so
the result preserves the intent of BOTH sides wherever they touch unrelated
code; where the two sides are genuinely incompatible, prefer the incoming
`origin/{{.Base}}` side and adapt this branch's change on top of it. Stage
every resolved file and conclude the merge with `git commit --no-edit` (never
a bare `git commit` or `git merge --continue` — both can open an interactive
editor, and this environment is headless). Then run the project's tests if it
has any and fix anything the merge itself broke. Do not expand scope beyond
making the merge conclude cleanly.

If a conflict cannot be resolved safely without a human decision (e.g. two
incompatible designs for the same behavior), leave the merge unresolved — do
NOT commit — and explain why. Do not run `git merge --abort`, `git reset`, or
otherwise back out the merge: leave MERGE_HEAD and the conflict markers in
place so a human can take over.

HEADLESS MODE: you cannot ask questions (AskUserQuestion is disabled). Make
reasonable calls and note them in the merge commit message.

Report the outcome in your final structured output: status "resolved" when the
merge was concluded with a merge commit, or status "blocked" with a
one-sentence reason in detail.
